            "count": 0,
            "bug_id": 19288,
            "text": "unserialize() and readRDS() read past the end of a string buffer when a version-3 stream declares the platform's wchar_t encoding (UCS-4LE/BE, or UTF-16LE on Windows) as its native encoding and holds a string that cannot be converted to UTF-8.\n\nReproducible example: attached as serialize-bound-recoding-repro.R; a normal build reads silently, while an AddressSanitizer build reports a dynamic-stack-buffer-overflow in wcslen(). This is OSS-Fuzz testcase 5161193048965120.\n\nAnalysis: ReadChar() in src/main/serialize.c hands the length-delimited string to reEnc3() to build the \"cannot be translated\" warning. reEnc3() infers from the encoding name that its input is a wide string and measures it with wcslen(), which runs past the buffer of length + 1 bytes.\n\nProposed patch: attached as serialize-bound-recoding.diff, against r90844. It adds reEnc4(), which takes the byte length explicitly, and uses it wherever the caller knows the length. It also makes unserialize() and infoRDS() reject an encoding name with an embedded nul, which is currently truncated silently, and adds a test to tests/reg-tests-1b.R. It supersedes attachment 3851 on the closed bug 19047, which was not applied.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/339, where CI results can be seen.",
            "creation_time": "2026-11-06T18:20:50Z",
            "is_private": false,
